[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE` disposition — exit status differed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout a closed pipe, the C binary was killed by signal 13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (shell status 141) while the Rust binary exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printf '1' | ./c_src/build/driver           | true   # -> 141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printf '1' | ./translation/target/.../driver | true   # -> 0    (before fix)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs; a C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program inherits the default `SIG_DFL`. The failed `write` therefore returned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `EPIPE` to Rust — where the error was discarded — instead of terminating the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m process.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` resets `SIGPIPE` to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIG_DFL` as the first statement of `main`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `broken_stdout_pipe_kills_both_with_sigpipe`, which closes the pipe's read end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *before* spawning the child so the first write always fails — no race.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This was the only behavioral divergence found. The items below were verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rather than repaired, and each is pinned by a test so a regression is caught.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors verified as already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `scanf("%d")` overflow: truncation, not saturation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc parses `%d` into a `long`, then the assignment to `int` truncates. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation reproduces both halves, including the saturation glibc applies when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the digits exceed `long`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Input | glibc `long` | Truncated `int` | Branch | Output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` | 2147483648 | `-2147483648` | `good()` | `5\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` | 4294967296 | `0` | **`bad()`** | `0\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `99999999999999999999` | `LONG_MAX` | `-1` | `good()` | `5\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-99999999999999999999` | `LONG_MIN` | `0` | **`bad()`** | `0\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The last two are the subtle ones: overflowing in one direction flips the branch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and in the other does not. Saturating to `i32` instead of truncating — a natural
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mistake in Rust — changes the output for `4294967296` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-99999999999999999999`. Confirmed caught by mutation (see below).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `scanf` reads across newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%d` skips *all* leading whitespace including `\n`, unlike `fgets`. So `"\n\n\n5"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints `5\n`, not the `bad()` output. The translation's whitespace skip uses the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m full C `isspace` set.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Matching failure performs no assignment
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m On `"abc"`, `scanf` returns 0 and does not write to `x`, which keeps its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m initializer `0`. The translation returns `None` and leaves `x` alone rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writing a sentinel. A lone `-` or `+` followed by EOF is also a matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failure, not a parse of zero — both reach `bad()`, so the distinction is not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m observable here, but it is modeled correctly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### The undefined behavior in `bad()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bad()` dereferences an uninitialized `int *`. This is genuine UB, so there is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m no "correct" value — only the reference build's behavior. The disassembly shows
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m why it is nonetheless stable:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bad():
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   sub    $0x10,%rsp
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   mov    -0x8(%rbp),%rax     # never written by bad(); leftover from scanf's frame
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   mov    %rax,%rdi
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   call   printIntPtrLine
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bad()` reads a stack slot it never writes, left behind by the preceding
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf` call, and that slot holds a pointer to a zero word. The reference build
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints `0\n` and exits 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because this depends on what `scanf` left on the stack, I checked whether it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m varies with anything an input could influence, since different inputs drive
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf` down different internal paths:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - all three `bad()`-reaching input classes (parsed zero, matching failure, EOF)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - 20 repeated identical runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - environment block sizes 0–5000 bytes and `argv` lengths 0–500 bytes, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   shift the initial stack pointer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - inputs up to 200 000 digits, which push glibc's `%d` conversion onto its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   wide-input path and change its stack and allocation behavior
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m It printed `0\n` in every case. `UNINITIALIZED_STACK_VALUE = 0` in `src/main.rs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m records that observation, with the bug preserved: `bad()` still prints an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "uninitialized" value rather than the `5` that `good()` returns.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Caveat worth stating plainly:** this value is a property of the reference
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build, not of the C language. Rebuilt with optimization, a different libc, or a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m different architecture, `bad()` could print anything or crash. The translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matches the build in `c_src/build`, which is what is being compared.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Test suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/tests/differential.rs` — 21 tests, roughly 800 input cases. Every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m test spawns **both** binaries as subprocesses and compares stdout bytes, stderr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes, exit code, *and* terminating signal. Nothing is loaded as a library;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m there is no `#[no_mangle]`, no `cdylib`, no `libloading`. No test is `#[ignore]`d,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m skipped or disabled. The C binary is built automatically by the harness if
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m absent, and a build failure is fatal rather than silently skipped — comparing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against a program that did not build measures nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Randomized sweeps use a fixed-seed xorshift PRNG, so any failure reproduces.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Suite validated by mutation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A test suite that passes is only meaningful if it can fail. Five faults were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m injected into the Rust source; each was caught, and the source was restored
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m afterwards:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Injected fault | Result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `bad()` prints `12345` instead of `0` | caught (18 tests) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Overflow saturates instead of truncating | caught (5 tests) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Whitespace skip excludes newlines | caught (2 tests) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `SIGPIPE` left as `SIG_IGN` | caught (1 test) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Exit status 1 instead of 0 | caught (20 tests) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The overflow and `SIGPIPE` mutations are the ones a stdout-only comparison would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m miss, which is why exit code and signal are asserted on every case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
